\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\,\\,2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$"